1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
You add another guardrail to an AI system. How much safer is

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
it? Here is a calculation that feels like an answer.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Take these miss rates. Multiply them. A reassuringly small result. The arithmetic

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:24,000
works. But listen to the assumption underneath it: independent failures.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:31,000
What if the guards miss the same things? What if they miss

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:38,000
completely different things? Their separate scores could look exactly the same.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Those separate scores alone do not tell us how often both guards

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,000
miss.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
This is an illustration: each guard is evaluated on the same inputs.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Keep your eyes on the scores. Watch what gets through both.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Each plate has the same miss rate. The scores are fixed. These

12
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,000
are possible arrangements, not measurements of a deployed system.

13
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Here, what gets past a guard is caught by the other. Now

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,000
move the misses together. Nothing on either scorecard changes. But the pile

15
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:27,000
underneath does.

16
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
The scores permit this whole interval. Independence selects the amber point. The

17
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,000
scores did not select it.

18
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
The missing information is which failures happen together. So where would you

19
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,000
look for it?

20
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Imagine a report with a column for each guard. Each column can

21
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:54,000
tell you how often that guard misses. But the question we just

22
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
asked belongs to a different column: how often do they all miss

23
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
the same item?

24
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,000
You cannot recover that column by making the individual columns more precise.

25
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Perfectly reported separate scores can still leave the overlap unknown. We just

26
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:23,000
watched the overlap move while the scores stood still.

27
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Missing joint reporting does not show that a stack performs badly. It

28
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
means this particular question has not been answered by the separate scores.

29
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,000
A blank is not a measured failure, and it is not a

30
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
measured success.

31
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,000
That distinction changes what we should go looking for. Not another impressive

32
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:50,000
individual score. A composition result, or shared item-level outcomes from which the

33
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,000
result can be computed. I looked for that evidence in a bounded

34
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,000
census of public reporting.

35
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:06,000
The census starts with a rule for what qualifies. It records a

36
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:13,000
source, a classification, and a reason. The counts come from those rows.

37
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Change a qualifying row and the count has to change with it.

38
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Watch the classifications become counts. Some records print a composition result. Some

39
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:33,000
release item outcomes that make a joint calculation possible. Those routes can

40
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:40,000
overlap; they are not buckets you can simply add.

41
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:46,000
The ladder asks progressively stricter questions about the evaluation basis and threshold

42
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:53,000
documentation. A stricter rung needs evidence that the earlier rung did not

43
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:00,000
require. Its label is part of its number.

44
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Here is the check running on the source file. The current envelope

45
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:13,000
is 20 records, 14 on a shared basis, and 5 with qualifying

46
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:20,000
joint evidence. The output is reproducible; the classifications remain open to challenge.

47
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:25,000
This is a bounded search of reporting, not proof that joint evidence

48
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,000
exists nowhere. It has a primary reviewer and declared limits. The result

49
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:35,000
is about what this search found under its rules, not a verdict

50
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:41,000
on every guardrail.

51
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:47,000
That is why the raw rows matter more than a dramatic headline.

52
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:53,000
You can inspect the sources, dispute a classification, or bring a qualifying

53
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:59,000
source the search missed. The count should survive those checks, or change.

54
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:04,000
And it did change. The census contains an earlier answer that no

55
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:09,000
longer stands as the main count. I want to show you the

56
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:15,000
moment its own rule rejected it.

57
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:20,000
The seal is the rule written before the count. Then a qualifying

58
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:26,000
row the search missed arrives. The rule already says what happens next.

59
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:38,000
The old answer is struck through. It stays readable. The replacement gets

60
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:44,000
its own place in the record. And the sensitivity readings keep their

61
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:50,000
own labels. They are not replacements for the main count.

62
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:54,000
There are different reasons to see an old number again. It may

63
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:59,000
be a historical result that has been superseded. Or it may be

64
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,000
the result of a separately declared sensitivity. Remove the labels and you

65
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:08,000
collapse different claims into a misleading numeral.

66
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,000
The correction does not establish that the revised census is complete. It

67
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:17,000
establishes what changed in this record and why. The next qualifying correction

68
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:22,000
must still be able to change the answer. Otherwise the rule would

69
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:27,000
only be decoration.

70
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:33,000
The old number is still in the log. That is not damage

71
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:39,000
control. That is the product. Now return to the missing column. What

72
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:45,000
happens when we actually have enough evidence to fill it?

73
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:49,000
For a released BELLS subset, the separate miss counts leave a set

74
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:54,000
of possible all-miss counts. The film constructs arrangements consistent with those constraints.

75
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:59,000
The moving arrangements illustrate feasible worlds; they are not the released item

76
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:04,000
rows.

77
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:13,000
The marginals permit 13 possible integer counts, from 0 through 12. A

78
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:22,000
precise list of individual results still leaves a set of joint answers.

79
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:28,000
Now add the union: the items caught by at least one guard.

80
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:34,000
Subtract those from the harmful-item total. The all-miss count is 9 of

81
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:40,000
82. This new evidence selects the count.

82
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:45,000
This result describes this released subset, not deployment risk. But notice the

83
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:50,000
difference: we did not make the uncertainty disappear by choosing a comforting

84
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:55,000
assumption. We supplied the missing constraint. That is what it takes to

85
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:00,000
turn this question into an answer.

86
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:04,000
So, how much safer? Separate scores alone do not identify the joint

87
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:09,000
failure rate.

88
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,000
If I missed a qualifying source, show me. If a calculation fails,

89
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:16,000
send the input and the result. The evidence and correction route are

90
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,000
below.

91
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:25,000
A claim you can check. A record that can change. That is

92
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:30,000
what I want to build here.
